     1	# Change log
     2	
     3	## 2026-09-29 — Tighten the compiled-GEMM Reference after Codex's second review
     4	
     5	Codex re-verified the GEMM-limit evidence (APPROVE WITH EDITS, no blocking findings; `ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/codex/verify-gemm-limits-r2.md`). The [[references/compiled-gemm-template-overflows-above-2-21-rows|compiled-GEMM Reference]] and `results.md` now:
     6	- separate the emitted `INDEX_DTYPE` (an all-buffer storage check) from the template size-arg dtype (output numel);
     7	- limit the "every failing graph" claim to kernels attributable from retained code, since two failures occurred during autotuning;
     8	- write L_in as floor(2³¹/K);
     9	- state the design bound as the strict < 2³¹ the guard implements.
    10	
    11	The run statement no longer implies Triton 3.5.0 was the installed version.
    12	
    13	## 2026-09-29 — Measure the compiled-GEMM limit at the rebuild's shapes (Codex-verified)
    14	
    15	A bounded probe ran on GPU 0 of the running pod (GEMM-limits run statement in `ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/run-statements/`). Codex verified it (`ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/codex/verify-gemm-limits-r1.md`, APPROVE WITH EDITS), and all its findings are applied. This revises the [[references/compiled-gemm-template-overflows-above-2-21-rows|compiled-GEMM Reference]]:
    16	- **Input-side overflow.** At these shapes the overflow is on the input side: the A-load wraps once M·K > 2³¹ while the template size argument stays int32. Input-wide GEMMs failed at L_in+1, and output-wide GEMMs stayed correct to M·N = 2³². The universal `M·max(K, N)` rule is withdrawn. M × max(in, out) remains the design bound, justified by backward reading forward outputs as inputs.
